EXT. TRAWLER "MARGARET ANN" - NORTH ATLANTIC - DAWN

Grey sea. Grey sky. A rust-streaked trawler, forty years old, wallows through a swell with nets hanging from her outriggers.

Salt. Wind. Gulls.

CAPTAIN DAVID VOSS, 60s, wind-cracked face, oilskins, stands in the wheelhouse door. His hands are scarred. His eyes are the color of the water.

On the deck below, LIN PARK, 20s, deckhand, in orange gear, hauls a net hand over hand. Her gloves are torn. She is on her first season.

INT. WHEELHOUSE - CONTINUOUS

Radar glow. A dented thermos. A paper chart pinned with a wrench.

Voss thumbs the radio.

COAST GUARD (V.O.)
(crackling)
Margaret Ann, Margaret Ann, this is Station Halvard. Come back.

VOSS
Halvard, Margaret Ann. Go ahead.

COAST GUARD (V.O.)
Captain, we have a gale warning, force nine, bearing two-seven-zero. Recommend you return to port.

VOSS
How long?

COAST GUARD (V.O.)
Four hours. Maybe less.

Voss sets the handset down. Nothing.

The sea heaves. Spray. Silence.

His phone on the dash lights up.

ON PHONE (TEXT): dad. please come home.

He turns it face-down.

EXT. DECK - CONTINUOUS

Lin lets the net drop, shaking out her hands.

LIN
Captain says anything?

NOELLE (O.S.)
(from the hold)
Not to me.

Nobody has seen Noelle in an hour.

Lin looks at the sky. A grey wall climbs the horizon.

This is the last time the Margaret Ann will fish these waters.

VOSS
(from above)
Cut the net, Lin.

LIN
There's six tons in it.

VOSS
Cut it.

She draws her knife. Rope. Salt. The first drops of rain.

The net falls away into the dark.
